{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"display_name":"Python 3","language":"python","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.13.15","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[],"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# 00 — Setup and data pipeline\n\n**Project:** Quantitatively Audited Explainable AI for Brain Tumor MRI Classification\n(with a multi-task segmentation extension)\n\nThis notebook handles everything that has to exist before any model is trained:\n\n1. install the few libraries Kaggle doesn't ship, and confirm the GPU is visible\n2. download the Figshare brain tumor dataset (Cheng et al., CC BY 4.0) into `/kaggle/working/`\n3. parse all 3064 `.mat` files into a single `manifest.csv` with a **patient-level** train/val/test split\n4. check the split for patient leakage and report class balance\n5. look at real images with their tumor masks overlaid, to confirm the masks line up\n\nIt trains nothing. It exists so that every later notebook (baseline, explanations, audit,\nuncertainty, multi-task, comparative audit) reads **the same manifest, the same split and the\nsame config**. Notebook 7 compares two models' explanation metrics, and that comparison only\nmeans something if both models were trained and evaluated on identical patients.\n\n**What this notebook writes to `/kaggle/working/`:**\n\n```\n/kaggle/working/\n├── configs/baseline_resnet18.yaml   # hyperparameters shared by all notebooks\n├── src/data/parse_mat.py            # .mat -> TumorSample reader\n├── src/utils/seed.py                # set_seed()\n├── data/mat/*.mat                   # 3064 extracted files\n├── data/raw/cvind.mat, README.txt   # kept for provenance\n├── manifest.csv                     # one row per image, with split column\n├── leakage_check.json\n└── run_info.json                    # library versions, counts, timestamp\n```","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 1. Dependencies\n\n`captum` (used for Integrated Gradients in notebook 2) is the only package that is actually\nmissing on current Kaggle images. `scikit-image`, `h5py` and `pyyaml` are usually already\ninstalled, so pip will just confirm them. They stay in the list so the notebook still runs if\nKaggle changes its base image.\n\nI'm not pinning versions here. I print them below and save them to `run_info.json` at the end,\nso any result can be traced back to the environment it came from. That's the part that matters\nfor a write-up.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"!pip install -q captum scikit-image h5py pyyaml","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T07:27:53.749225Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T07:27:53.749533Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.531589Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T07:27:53.749503Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.5306Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":38},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import json\nimport hashlib\nimport shutil\nimport sys\nimport zipfile\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport h5py\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport requests\nimport sklearn\nimport skimage\nimport torch\nimport torchvision\nimport captum\nimport yaml\nfrom skimage import measure\nfrom sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split\nfrom tqdm.auto import tqdm\n\nVERSIONS = {\n    \"python\": sys.version.split()[0],\n    \"torch\": torch.__version__,\n    \"torchvision\": torchvision.__version__,\n    \"captum\": captum.__version__,\n    \"sklearn\": sklearn.__version__,\n    \"skimage\": skimage.__version__,\n    \"h5py\": h5py.__version__,\n    \"numpy\": np.__version__,\n    \"pandas\": pd.__version__,\n}\nfor k, v in VERSIONS.items():\n    print(f\"{k:12s} {v}\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.533036Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.533423Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.541981Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.53339Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.540984Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"python       3.13.15\ntorch        2.11.0+cpu\ntorchvision  0.26.0+cpu\ncaptum       0.9.0\nsklearn      1.6.1\nskimage      0.25.2\nh5py         3.16.0\nnumpy        2.1.3\npandas       2.3.3\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":39},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 2. GPU check\n\nThis notebook doesn't need a GPU, since it only does file I/O and plotting. I still check here\nso that a missing accelerator shows up now and not 20 minutes into notebook 1.\n\nIf Kaggle gives you **T4 x2**, later notebooks use one GPU only. ResNet18 on ~2k training images\nis small enough that `DataParallel` would add overhead and complicate the hooks Grad-CAM needs\n(notebook 2). It wouldn't save meaningful time.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"print(\"CUDA available:\", torch.cuda.is_available())\nif torch.cuda.is_available():\n    DEVICE = torch.device(\"cuda\")\n    for i in range(torch.cuda.device_count()):\n        print(f\"  cuda:{i}  {torch.cuda.get_device_name(i)}\")\n    print(\"CUDA runtime:\", torch.version.cuda, \"| cuDNN:\", torch.backends.cudnn.version())\nelse:\n    DEVICE = torch.device(\"cpu\")\n    print(\"No GPU. Fine for this notebook; enable the accelerator before notebook 1.\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.543189Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.543461Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.566302Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.543437Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.565375Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"CUDA available: False\nNo GPU. Fine for this notebook; enable the accelerator before notebook 1.\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":40},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 3. Project layout, config, and seeding\n\nEverything lives under `/kaggle/working/` because that's the only writable directory whose\ncontents survive a **Save Version**.\n\nAll paths stored in the manifest are **relative to `DATA_ROOT`**, never absolute. When notebook 1\nattaches this notebook's output as an input, it will be mounted read-only somewhere like\n`/kaggle/input/<this-notebook-slug>/`, not `/kaggle/working/`. With relative paths, later\nnotebooks only have to change one `DATA_ROOT` line.\n\nI also write the two small helper modules (`parse_mat.py`, `seed.py`) to `src/` with `%%writefile`.\nThey become part of this notebook's output, so later notebooks can import exactly the same code\nand not paste copies that drift apart over time.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"WORK = Path(\"/kaggle/working\")\nDATA_ROOT = WORK / \"data\"\nRAW_DIR = DATA_ROOT / \"raw\"   # zips while downloading, then just cvind.mat + README\nMAT_DIR = DATA_ROOT / \"mat\"   # the 3064 .mat files, flat\n\nfor d in [RAW_DIR, MAT_DIR, WORK / \"configs\", WORK / \"src\" / \"data\", WORK / \"src\" / \"utils\", WORK / \"results\"]:\n    d.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\nfor pkg in [WORK / \"src\", WORK / \"src\" / \"data\", WORK / \"src\" / \"utils\"]:\n    (pkg / \"__init__.py\").touch()\n\nif str(WORK) not in sys.path:\n    sys.path.insert(0, str(WORK))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.56754Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.567903Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.584825Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.567868Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.58388Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":41},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 3a. Config\n\nThese are the values from `configs/baseline_resnet18.yaml`. **If your local YAML has fields I\nhaven't listed here (optimizer, weight decay, scheduler, num_workers, ...), paste your full\nfile over this cell.** The YAML is the source of truth; this cell just puts a copy where Kaggle\ncan see it.\n\nEvery downstream notebook loads its hyperparameters from this file and never hard-codes them,\nso the baseline and the multi-task model in notebook 6 can't quietly end up with different\nsettings.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"%%writefile /kaggle/working/configs/baseline_resnet18.yaml\nseed: 42\nimage_size: 224\nbatch_size: 32\n\ndata:\n  manifest: manifest.csv        # relative to DATA_ROOT's parent (the notebook output dir)\n  class_names: [meningioma, glioma, pituitary]   # index = raw label - 1\n  split: {train: 0.70, val: 0.15, test: 0.15}\n\nmodel:\n  architecture: resnet18\n  dropout_p: 0.3\n\ntrain:\n  epochs: 30\n  lr: 0.0003\n\nexplain:\n  methods: [gradcam, integrated_gradients]\n  target_layer: layer4\n\naudit:\n  deletion_insertion_steps: 20\n  stability_transforms:\n    - {type: rotate, degrees: 5}\n    - {type: rotate, degrees: -5}\n    - {type: brightness, factor: 1.10}\n  localization_threshold: 0.5\n\nuncertainty:\n  n_forward_passes: 30","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.58602Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.586406Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.593641Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.586369Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.592877Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Overwriting /kaggle/working/configs/baseline_resnet18.yaml\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":42},{"cell_type":"code","source":"with open(WORK / \"configs\" / \"baseline_resnet18.yaml\") as f:\n    CFG = yaml.safe_load(f)\n\nSEED = CFG[\"seed\"]\nCLASS_NAMES = CFG[\"data\"][\"class_names\"]\nprint(json.dumps(CFG, indent=2))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.59471Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.595027Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.620777Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.594994Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.620021Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"{\n  \"seed\": 42,\n  \"image_size\": 224,\n  \"batch_size\": 32,\n  \"data\": {\n    \"manifest\": \"manifest.csv\",\n    \"class_names\": [\n      \"meningioma\",\n      \"glioma\",\n      \"pituitary\"\n    ],\n    \"split\": {\n      \"train\": 0.7,\n      \"val\": 0.15,\n      \"test\": 0.15\n    }\n  },\n  \"model\": {\n    \"architecture\": \"resnet18\",\n    \"dropout_p\": 0.3\n  },\n  \"train\": {\n    \"epochs\": 30,\n    \"lr\": 0.0003\n  },\n  \"explain\": {\n    \"methods\": [\n      \"gradcam\",\n      \"integrated_gradients\"\n    ],\n    \"target_layer\": \"layer4\"\n  },\n  \"audit\": {\n    \"deletion_insertion_steps\": 20,\n    \"stability_transforms\": [\n      {\n        \"type\": \"rotate\",\n        \"degrees\": 5\n      },\n      {\n        \"type\": \"rotate\",\n        \"degrees\": -5\n      },\n      {\n        \"type\": \"brightness\",\n        \"factor\": 1.1\n      }\n    ],\n    \"localization_threshold\": 0.5\n  },\n  \"uncertainty\": {\n    \"n_forward_passes\": 30\n  }\n}\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":43},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### 3b. Seeding\n\nNothing in this notebook is stochastic except the split and which images get plotted, and both\nof those take `SEED` explicitly. The seed module is written here so notebooks 1–7 can share it.\n\n`cudnn.deterministic=True` / `benchmark=False` costs a little training speed. I'll take that\ntrade: the audit in notebook 3 compares metric values between models, and I'd rather two runs\nof the same model give the same numbers than save a few minutes per epoch.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"%%writefile /kaggle/working/src/utils/seed.py\nimport os\nimport random\n\nimport numpy as np\nimport torch\n\n\ndef set_seed(seed: int = 42, deterministic: bool = True) -> None:\n    random.seed(seed)\n    np.random.seed(seed)\n    torch.manual_seed(seed)\n    torch.cuda.manual_seed_all(seed)\n    # only affects subprocesses (e.g. DataLoader workers); the current interpreter's hash seed is already fixed\n    os.environ[\"PYTHONHASHSEED\"] = str(seed)\n    if deterministic:\n        torch.backends.cudnn.deterministic = True\n        torch.backends.cudnn.benchmark = False","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.62201Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.622352Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.635051Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.622316Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.634197Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Overwriting /kaggle/working/src/utils/seed.py\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":44},{"cell_type":"code","source":"from src.utils.seed import set_seed\n\nset_seed(SEED)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.636126Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.636456Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.650649Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.636422Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.649866Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":45},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 4. Download the dataset from Figshare\n\nSource: Cheng, J. *Brain tumor dataset*, Figshare, article 1512427 (CC BY 4.0). The article has\nfour zip parts (`brainTumorDataPublic_1-766.zip`, ..., `_2299-3064.zip`), `cvind.mat` (the\nauthors' 5-fold CV indices) and a `README.txt`.\n\nUnlike `scripts/download_data.py`, which has the `ndownloader.figshare.com` URLs hard-coded, I\nask the Figshare API for the file list. The response also includes each file's size and MD5,\nso I can verify each download against Figshare's own checksum. A truncated zip would otherwise\nonly show up as a confusing `BadZipFile` later.\n\nIf the API call fails (Figshare sometimes rate-limits cloud IPs), paste the URL dict from\n`scripts/download_data.py` into `FALLBACK_URLS` and re-run; the MD5 check is skipped in that case.\n\nThe cell skips everything if the 3064 `.mat` files are already extracted, so it's safe to re-run.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"ARTICLE_ID = 1512427\nN_EXPECTED = 3064\nFALLBACK_URLS = {}   # {\"filename\": \"https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/<id>\", ...} from download_data.py\n\n# a plain requests UA occasionally gets a 403 from Figshare's CDN\nHEADERS = {\"User-Agent\": \"Mozilla/5.0 (research download; brain-tumor-xai-audit)\"}\n\n\ndef md5sum(path, chunk=1 << 20):\n    h = hashlib.md5()\n    with open(path, \"rb\") as fh:\n        for block in iter(lambda: fh.read(chunk), b\"\"):\n            h.update(block)\n    return h.hexdigest()\n\n\ndef download(url, dest, size=None, md5=None):\n    if dest.exists() and (md5 is None or md5sum(dest) == md5):\n        print(f\"have {dest.name}\")\n        return\n    tmp = dest.with_name(dest.name + \".part\")   # so an interrupted download never looks complete\n    with requests.get(url, stream=True, timeout=60, headers=HEADERS) as r:\n        r.raise_for_status()\n        with open(tmp, \"wb\") as fh, tqdm(total=size, unit=\"B\", unit_scale=True, desc=dest.name) as bar:\n            for block in r.iter_content(chunk_size=1 << 20):\n                fh.write(block)\n                bar.update(len(block))\n    if md5 is not None and md5sum(tmp) != md5:\n        tmp.unlink()\n        raise RuntimeError(f\"MD5 mismatch for {dest.name}; re-run the cell\")\n    tmp.rename(dest)\n\n\nif len(list(MAT_DIR.glob(\"*.mat\"))) == N_EXPECTED:\n    print(f\"{N_EXPECTED} .mat files already extracted, skipping download\")\nelse:\n    if FALLBACK_URLS:\n        files = [{\"name\": n, \"download_url\": u, \"size\": None, \"computed_md5\": None} for n, u in FALLBACK_URLS.items()]\n    else:\n        resp = requests.get(f\"https://api.figshare.com/v2/articles/{ARTICLE_ID}/files\", timeout=30, headers=HEADERS)\n        resp.raise_for_status()\n        files = resp.json()\n\n    for f in files:\n        size_mb = f[\"size\"] / 1e6 if f[\"size\"] else float(\"nan\")\n        print(f\"{f['name']:40s} {size_mb:8.1f} MB\")\n    for f in files:\n        download(f[\"download_url\"], RAW_DIR / f[\"name\"], size=f[\"size\"], md5=f[\"computed_md5\"])","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.651755Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.652086Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.674666Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.652051Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.67382Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"3064 .mat files already extracted, skipping download\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":46},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 5. Extract\n\nThe four zips go into one flat `data/mat/` directory. Filenames are `1.mat` ... `3064.mat` across\nall parts, so flattening is safe. I still check the final count below rather than assume it,\nbecause a silent filename collision would cost images without any error.\n\nThe zips are deleted after extraction. They're the same data twice over, and everything in\n`/kaggle/working/` counts toward the saved version's output size. `cvind.mat` and the README stay\nfor provenance; the README documents the `.mat` field layout that `parse_mat.py` depends on.\n\nI don't use `cvind.mat` for splitting. The project plan calls for one held-out 70/15/15 split\n(the audit and uncertainty notebooks need one fixed test set, not five folds), and I want the\npatient grouping to be something I built and checked myself (section 7).","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"for zp in sorted(RAW_DIR.glob(\"*.zip\")):\n    with zipfile.ZipFile(zp) as zf:\n        members = [m for m in zf.infolist() if m.filename.endswith(\".mat\")]\n        for m in tqdm(members, desc=zp.name, leave=False):\n            # flatten whatever internal folder structure the zip has\n            target = MAT_DIR / Path(m.filename).name\n            if not target.exists():\n                with zf.open(m) as src, open(target, \"wb\") as dst:\n                    shutil.copyfileobj(src, dst)\n    zp.unlink()\n\nmat_files = sorted(MAT_DIR.glob(\"*.mat\"), key=lambda p: int(p.stem))\nprint(len(mat_files), \"mat files\")\nassert len(mat_files) == N_EXPECTED, f\"expected {N_EXPECTED}, got {len(mat_files)}\"\nassert [int(p.stem) for p in mat_files] == list(range(1, N_EXPECTED + 1))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.675641Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.67595Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.711817Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.675926Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.711077Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"3064 mat files\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":47},{"cell_type":"code","source":"# the README is short and documents the field layout -- worth reading once\nreadme = next(RAW_DIR.glob(\"README*\"), None)\nprint(readme.read_text(errors=\"replace\") if readme else \"README not found\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.712784Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.713111Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.718983Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.713075Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.718239Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"This brain tumor dataset containing 3064 T1-weighted contrast-inhanced images from 233 patients with three kinds of brain tumor: meningioma (708 slices), glioma (1426 slices), and pituitary tumor (930 slices). Due to the file size limit of repository, we split the whole dataset into 4 subsets, and achive them in 4 .zip files with each .zip file containing 766 slices.The 5-fold cross-validation indices are also provided (cvind.mat).\n\n1. Data structure\nThis data is organized in matlab data format (.mat file). Each file stores a struct containing the following fields for an image:\n\ncjdata.label: 1 for meningioma, 2 for glioma, 3 for pituitary tumor\ncjdata.PID: patient ID\ncjdata.image: image data\ncjdata.tumorBorder: a vector storing the coordinates of discrete points on tumor border. For example, in [x1, y1, x2, y2,...], (xi, yi) are planar coordinates on the tumor border. They were generated by manually delineating the tumor border. So we can use it to generate binary image of tumor mask.\ncjdata.tumorMask: a binary image with 1s indicating the tumor region\n\n2. How to convert .mat format to other image formats?\nFor example, you can use the MATLAB code below to convert the .mat images to .jpg images (or other formats by simply changing jpg to the format you want). The resulting jpg images will be stored in three folders, each for one class. \n\ninput_folder = 'your input path';\noutput_folder = 'your output path';\nfile_list = dir(fullfile(input_folder, '*.mat')); \nfor i = 1:numel(file_list)\n    file_name = file_list(i).name;\n    file_path = fullfile(input_folder, file_name);\n    \n    mat_data = load(file_path);  \n    cjdata = mat_data.cjdata;\n    \n    im1 = double(cjdata.image); \n    min1 = min(im1(:));\n    max1 = max(im1(:));\n    im = uint8(255/(max1-min1)*(im1-min1));\n    label = cjdata.label;  \n    \n    label_folder = fullfile(output_folder, num2str(label));\n    if ~exist(label_folder, 'dir')\n        mkdir(label_folder); \n    end\n    \n    [~, file_name_base, ~] = fileparts(file_name);\n    output_file_path = fullfile(label_folder, strcat(file_name_base, '.jpg'));\n    \n    imwrite(im, output_file_path);  \nend\n\n3. The acquisition protocol used to acquire the images\nAll images in our dataset (T1-weighted contrast-enhanced MRI) were acquired after Gd-DTPA injection at Nanfang Hospital, Guangzhou, China and General Hospital, Tianjin Medical University, China from 2005.9 to 2010.10. The images have an in-plane resolution of 512 × 512 with pixel dimensions of 0.49 × 0.49 mm^2 . The slice thickness is 6 mm and the slice gap is 1 mm. The Gd dose was 0.1 mmol/kg at a rate of 2 ml/s. \n\nNo further details are available due to the long passage of time. \n\n4. Citations\nThis data was used in the following papers:\n1. Cheng, Jun, et al. \"Enhanced Performance of Brain Tumor Classification via Tumor Region Augmentation and Partition.\" PloS one 10.10 (2015).\n2. Cheng, Jun, et al. \"Retrieval of Brain Tumors by Adaptive Spatial Pooling and Fisher Vector Representation.\" PloS one 11.6 (2016). Matlab source codes are available on github https://github.com/chengjun583/brainTumorRetrieval\n\n5. Contact information\nJun Cheng\nSchool of Biomedical Engineering\nShenzhen University, Shenzhen, China\nEmail: chengjun583@qq.com\n\n\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":48},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 6. Parsing one `.mat` file\n\nThe files are MATLAB v7.3, which is HDF5 underneath, so `scipy.io.loadmat` can't read them and\n`h5py` can. Two things are less obvious than they look:\n\n- **Transposes.** MATLAB stores arrays column-major; h5py hands them back with the axes reversed.\n  For a 512×512 image you'd never notice from the shape, but the anatomy would be mirrored across\n  the diagonal compared with how the images look in MATLAB/the paper. `.T` on both the image\n  and the mask fixes it, and since both get the same treatment they stay aligned either way.\n- **PID is a char array.** It arrives as a column of uint16 code points and has to be decoded\n  back into a string. Some PIDs have trailing whitespace, hence the `.strip()`; without it, the\n  same patient could end up counted as two \"different\" patients, which would break the\n  leakage check.\n\n`tumorBorder` is a flat `[x1, y1, x2, y2, ...]` vector according to the README. I reshape it to\n`(K, 2)` and assume `(row, col)` pairs. The overlay in section 8 is what actually checks that.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"%%writefile /kaggle/working/src/data/parse_mat.py\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\nimport h5py\nimport numpy as np\n\nLABEL_NAMES = {1: \"meningioma\", 2: \"glioma\", 3: \"pituitary\"}\n\n\n@dataclass\nclass TumorSample:\n    label: int                # raw 1/2/3\n    patient_id: str\n    image: np.ndarray         # (H, W) int16\n    tumor_mask: np.ndarray    # (H, W) uint8 in {0, 1}\n    tumor_border: np.ndarray  # (K, 2) polygon vertices, assumed (row, col)\n\n\ndef _decode_chars(ds) -> str:\n    # MATLAB char arrays come through h5py as uint16 code points\n    return \"\".join(chr(int(c)) for c in np.asarray(ds).ravel())\n\n\ndef read_mat(path) -> TumorSample:\n    with h5py.File(path, \"r\") as f:\n        cj = f[\"cjdata\"]\n        # h5py exposes MATLAB's column-major arrays with axes reversed\n        image = np.asarray(cj[\"image\"]).T\n        mask = np.asarray(cj[\"tumorMask\"]).T.astype(np.uint8)\n        return TumorSample(\n            label=int(np.asarray(cj[\"label\"]).item()),\n            patient_id=_decode_chars(cj[\"PID\"]).strip(),\n            image=image,\n            tumor_mask=mask,\n            tumor_border=np.asarray(cj[\"tumorBorder\"]).ravel().reshape(-1, 2),\n        )","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.720242Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.720562Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.735238Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.720535Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.734443Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Overwriting /kaggle/working/src/data/parse_mat.py\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":49},{"cell_type":"code","source":"from src.data.parse_mat import read_mat, LABEL_NAMES, TumorSample\n\ns = read_mat(mat_files[0])\nprint(\"label      \", s.label, LABEL_NAMES[s.label])\nprint(\"patient_id \", repr(s.patient_id))\nprint(\"image      \", s.image.shape, s.image.dtype, \"range\", s.image.min(), s.image.max())\nprint(\"tumor_mask \", s.tumor_mask.shape, np.unique(s.tumor_mask), \"pixels:\", int(s.tumor_mask.sum()))\nprint(\"border     \", s.tumor_border.shape)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.736345Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.737016Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.768452Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.73699Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.767633Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"label       1 meningioma\npatient_id  '100360'\nimage       (512, 512) int16 range 0 3366\ntumor_mask  (512, 512) [0 1] pixels: 4569\nborder      (19, 2)\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":50},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 7. Manifest and patient-level split\n\n### Why split by patient\n\nEach patient contributes several slices (glioma patients especially), and neighbouring slices\nfrom one scan look almost the same. If images were split at random, the test set would hold\nnear-copies of training images, accuracy would be inflated, and, worse for this project, the\nexplanation audit would partly be measuring memorisation of specific patients' anatomy. So\n**patients** are assigned to splits, and all of a patient's images follow them.\n\n### How\n\n1. Parse every file once, recording label, patient ID, tumor pixel count and image size.\n2. Confirm every patient has exactly one label (verified before on your machine; re-checked here\n   because this is a fresh download).\n3. Build a one-row-per-patient table and split it **70 / 15 / 15**, stratified by label, with\n   two calls to `train_test_split`: 70 vs 30, then the 30 split in half.\n\nOne caveat: stratification balances **patients** per class, not **images**. Patients contribute\ndifferent numbers of slices, so the image-level fractions will drift a little from 70/15/15.\nI print both below so the drift is visible.\n\nThe parse loop reads every image in full, about a minute. That's deliberate: it also checks that\nevery file opens and that each mask's shape matches its image, before any training depends on it.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"rows = []\nfor p in tqdm(mat_files, desc=\"parsing\"):\n    s = read_mat(p)\n    assert s.tumor_mask.shape == s.image.shape, p.name\n    rows.append({\n        \"file_path\": str(p.relative_to(DATA_ROOT)),\n        \"label\": s.label,\n        \"label_name\": LABEL_NAMES[s.label],\n        \"patient_id\": s.patient_id,\n        \"tumor_pixel_count\": int(s.tumor_mask.sum()),\n        \"image_h\": s.image.shape[0],\n        \"image_w\": s.image.shape[1],\n    })\n\nmanifest = pd.DataFrame(rows)\nprint(manifest.shape)\nprint(manifest.label_name.value_counts().reindex(CLASS_NAMES))\nprint(\"\\nimage sizes:\")\nprint(manifest.groupby([\"image_h\", \"image_w\"]).size())\nprint(\"\\nimages with an empty mask:\", int((manifest.tumor_pixel_count == 0).sum()))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.769603Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.769966Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T07:28:16.59106Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T07:27:57.769938Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T07:28:16.590216Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"parsing:   0%|          | 0/3064 [00:00<?, ?it/s]","application/vnd.jupyter.widget-view+json":{"version_major":2,"version_minor":0,"model_id":"b8f1a5d1dd8c4dc0879e0a1da8682f90"}},"metadata":{}},{"name":"stdout","text":"(3064, 7)\nlabel_name\nmeningioma     708\nglioma        1426\npituitary      930\nName: count, dtype: int64\n\nimage sizes:\nimage_h  image_w\n256      256          15\n512      512        3049\ndtype: int64\n\nimages with an empty mask: 0\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":51},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Expected: 708 / 1426 / 930 for meningioma / glioma / pituitary.\n\nMost images are 512×512, but a small number are 256×256. That's why the manifest keeps `image_h`\n/ `image_w`. The resize to 224 in the transforms makes this invisible to the model, but tumor\npixel counts aren't comparable across the two sizes without normalising by area (section 9 does that).","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"labels_per_patient = manifest.groupby(\"patient_id\")[\"label\"].nunique()\nassert (labels_per_patient == 1).all(), labels_per_patient[labels_per_patient > 1]\n\npatients = (\n    manifest.groupby(\"patient_id\")\n    .agg(label=(\"label\", \"first\"), n_images=(\"file_path\", \"size\"))\n    .reset_index()\n)\nprint(f\"{len(patients)} patients (expected 233), every one with a single label\")\nprint(patients.groupby(\"label\").agg(patients=(\"patient_id\", \"size\"), images=(\"n_images\", \"sum\"),\n                                    median_slices=(\"n_images\", \"median\")))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T07:28:16.592224Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T07:28:16.592501Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T07:28:16.615192Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T07:28:16.592475Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T07:28:16.614344Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"233 patients (expected 233), every one with a single label\n       patients  images  median_slices\nlabel                                 \n1            82     708            6.5\n2            89    1426           17.0\n3            62     930           15.0\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":52},{"cell_type":"code","source":"fr = CFG[\"data\"][\"split\"]\ntrain_p, rest_p = train_test_split(\n    patients, test_size=fr[\"val\"] + fr[\"test\"], stratify=patients.label, random_state=SEED\n)\nval_p, test_p = train_test_split(\n    rest_p, test_size=fr[\"test\"] / (fr[\"val\"] + fr[\"test\"]), stratify=rest_p.label, random_state=SEED\n)\n# TODO: if the image-level test fraction lands far from 15%, try StratifiedGroupKFold-style\n# assignment that balances images too -- the simple two-stage split was close enough last time\n\nsplit_of = {pid: name for name, part in [(\"train\", train_p), (\"val\", val_p), (\"test\", test_p)]\n            for pid in part.patient_id}\nmanifest[\"split\"] = manifest.patient_id.map(split_of)\nassert manifest.split.notna().all()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T07:28:16.616192Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T07:28:16.616455Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T07:28:16.630725Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T07:28:16.616431Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T07:28:16.62974Z"}},"outputs":[],"execution_count":53},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Leakage check\n\nThree things have to hold:\n\n- no patient ID appears in more than one split\n- no file appears more than once in the manifest\n- every patient is assigned somewhere\n\nThe result goes into `leakage_check.json`, so a later notebook (or a reviewer) can confirm the\nsplit it's using passed, and doesn't have to take my word for it.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"pid_sets = {name: set(manifest.loc[manifest.split == name, \"patient_id\"]) for name in [\"train\", \"val\", \"test\"]}\noverlaps = {\n    \"train&val\": sorted(pid_sets[\"train\"] & pid_sets[\"val\"]),\n    \"train&test\": sorted(pid_sets[\"train\"] & pid_sets[\"test\"]),\n    \"val&test\": sorted(pid_sets[\"val\"] & pid_sets[\"test\"]),\n}\nleakage = {\n    \"patient_overlaps\": overlaps,\n    \"duplicate_files\": int(manifest.file_path.duplicated().sum()),\n    \"n_patients_per_split\": {k: len(v) for k, v in pid_sets.items()},\n    \"all_patients_assigned\": sum(len(v) for v in pid_sets.values()) == len(patients),\n}\nleakage[\"passed\"] = (\n    all(len(v) == 0 for v in overlaps.values())\n    and leakage[\"duplicate_files\"] == 0\n    and leakage[\"all_patients_assigned\"]\n)\nprint(json.dumps(leakage, indent=2))\nassert leakage[\"passed\"], \"patient leakage between splits\"\n\nwith open(WORK / \"leakage_check.json\", \"w\") as f:\n    json.dump(leakage, f, indent=2)","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T07:28:16.635682Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T07:28:16.636069Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T07:28:16.65817Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T07:28:16.636041Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T07:28:16.657165Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"{\n  \"patient_overlaps\": {\n    \"train&val\": [],\n    \"train&test\": [],\n    \"val&test\": []\n  },\n  \"duplicate_files\": 0,\n  \"n_patients_per_split\": {\n    \"train\": 163,\n    \"val\": 35,\n    \"test\": 35\n  },\n  \"all_patients_assigned\": true,\n  \"passed\": true\n}\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":54},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"### Class balance per split\n\nThe first table shows image counts, the second shows each split's class proportions. The\nproportions should be close across rows; if test had noticeably fewer meningiomas than train,\nper-class audit metrics in notebook 3 would have wider error bars for that class. The last table\nis the patient view, where stratification is exact up to rounding.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"order = [\"train\", \"val\", \"test\"]\ncounts = pd.crosstab(manifest.split, manifest.label_name).reindex(index=order, columns=CLASS_NAMES)\ncounts[\"total\"] = counts.sum(axis=1)\ncounts[\"frac_of_images\"] = (counts.total / counts.total.sum()).round(3)\ndisplay(counts)\n\ndisplay(pd.crosstab(manifest.split, manifest.label_name, normalize=\"index\")\n        .reindex(index=order, columns=CLASS_NAMES).round(3))\n\ndisplay(pd.crosstab(manifest.drop_duplicates(\"patient_id\").split,\n                    manifest.drop_duplicates(\"patient_id\").label_name)\n        .reindex(index=order, columns=CLASS_NAMES).rename_axis(\"patients\"))","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T07:28:16.659411Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T07:28:16.6597Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T07:28:16.70997Z","shell.execute_reply.started"