 </thead>\n  <tbody>\n    <tr>\n      <th>0</th>\n      <td>mat/1.mat</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>meningioma</td>\n      <td>100360</td>\n      <td>4569</td>\n      <td>512</td>\n      <td>512</td>\n      <td>train</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>1</th>\n      <td>mat/2.mat</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>meningioma</td>\n      <td>100360</td>\n      <td>8630</td>\n      <td>512</td>\n      <td>512</td>\n      <td>train</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>2</th>\n      <td>mat/3.mat</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>meningioma</td>\n      <td>100360</td>\n      <td>10511</td>\n      <td>512</td>\n      <td>512</td>\n      <td>train</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>3</th>\n      <td>mat/4.mat</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>meningioma</td>\n      <td>100360</td>\n      <td>8897</td>\n      <td>512</td>\n      <td>512</td>\n      <td>train</td>\n    </tr>\n    <tr>\n      <th>4</th>\n      <td>mat/5.mat</td>\n      <td>1</td>\n      <td>meningioma</td>\n      <td>100360</td>\n      <td>11250</td>\n      <td>512</td>\n      <td>512</td>\n      <td>train</td>\n    </tr>\n  </tbody>\n</table>\n</div>"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":56},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 8. Visual sanity check\n\nFour random images per class with:\n\n- **red**: contour of `tumorMask` (via `skimage.measure.find_contours` at 0.5)\n- **cyan dots**: the `tumorBorder` polygon vertices, under the `(row, col)` assumption\n\nThings to look for:\n\n1. The red contour sits on a visible lesion. This is the check that matters most: notebooks 3\n   and 7 score explanation heatmaps against these masks, and a transposed or shifted mask would\n   make every localization number meaningless while still producing plausible-looking values.\n2. The cyan dots trace the red contour. If they trace a *mirrored* copy (reflected across the\n   diagonal), the border pairs are `(col, row)`; swap the columns in `parse_mat.py`. Nothing\n   downstream uses the border yet, so this only matters if it's used later.\n3. Anatomy is the right way up and consistent across images. Pituitary tumors should sit near the\n   midline at the skull base, and meningiomas usually against the skull or dura.\n\nImages are shown with the same per-image min-max scaling `BrainTumorDataset` applies, so this\nis the intensity range the model actually receives (before the resize to 224).","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"def to_uint8(img):\n    # same per-image min-max as BrainTumorDataset\n    img = img.astype(np.float32)\n    lo, hi = img.min(), img.max()\n    return ((img - lo) / (hi - lo + 1e-8) * 255).astype(np.uint8)\n\n\nN_PER_CLASS = 4\nrng = np.random.default_rng(SEED)\n\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(len(CLASS_NAMES), N_PER_CLASS, figsize=(3.3 * N_PER_CLASS, 3.5 * len(CLASS_NAMES)))\nfor row, cname in enumerate(CLASS_NAMES):\n    sub = manifest[manifest.label_name == cname]\n    picks = sub.iloc[rng.choice(len(sub), N_PER_CLASS, replace=False)]\n    for ax, (_, r) in zip(axes[row], picks.iterrows()):\n        s = read_mat(DATA_ROOT / r.file_path)\n        ax.imshow(to_uint8(s.image), cmap=\"gray\")\n        for c in measure.find_contours(s.tumor_mask, 0.5):\n            ax.plot(c[:, 1], c[:, 0], color=\"red\", lw=1.2)\n        ax.scatter(s.tumor_border[:, 1], s.tumor_border[:, 0], s=1.5, c=\"cyan\")\n        ax.set_title(f\"{cname} | pid {r.patient_id}\\n{Path(r.file_path).name} | {r.split} | {r.image_h}px\", fontsize=8)\n        ax.axis(\"off\")\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T07:28:16.741145Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T07:28:16.741483Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T07:28:18.614943Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T07:28:16.741445Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T07:28:18.613816Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1320x1050 with 12 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":57},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"The same check on the hardest cases: the three smallest tumors per class, measured as a fraction\nof image area. If a mask were off by a few pixels, these are the images where it would show,\nand they are also where Grad-CAM's coarse 7×7 `layer4` resolution will struggle most at\nlocalization (worth remembering when reading notebook 3's results).","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"manifest[\"tumor_frac\"] = manifest.tumor_pixel_count / (manifest.image_h * manifest.image_w)\n\nfig, axes = plt.subplots(len(CLASS_NAMES), 3, figsize=(10, 3.5 * len(CLASS_NAMES)))\nfor row, cname in enumerate(CLASS_NAMES):\n    smallest = manifest[manifest.label_name == cname].nsmallest(3, \"tumor_frac\")\n    for ax, (_, r) in zip(axes[row], smallest.iterrows()):\n        s = read_mat(DATA_ROOT / r.file_path)\n        ax.imshow(to_uint8(s.image), cmap=\"gray\")\n        for c in measure.find_contours(s.tumor_mask, 0.5):\n            ax.plot(c[:, 1], c[:, 0], color=\"red\", lw=1.2)\n        ax.set_title(f\"{cname} | {Path(r.file_path).name}\\ntumor = {100 * r.tumor_frac:.2f}% of image\", fontsize=8)\n        ax.axis(\"off\")\nplt.tight_layout()\nplt.show()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T07:28:18.616884Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T07:28:18.617191Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T07:28:19.989342Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T07:28:18.617166Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T07:28:19.987465Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 1000x1050 with 9 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}}],"execution_count":58},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 9. Tumor size distribution\n\nThis isn't strictly part of data setup, but it affects how notebook 3's localization metric\nshould be read. If pituitary tumors cover far less of the image than gliomas, then a heatmap\nthresholded at `localization_threshold = 0.5` will look worse on pituitary even if the model\nattends to the right place, because a blob from a 7×7 map is larger than the tumor. Seeing the\ndistribution now means that per-class differences later aren't misread as differences in model\nbehaviour.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7, 3.5))\nbins = np.linspace(0, manifest.tumor_frac.quantile(0.99), 50)\nfor cname in CLASS_NAMES:\n    ax.hist(manifest.loc[manifest.label_name == cname, \"tumor_frac\"], bins=bins, alpha=0.55, label=cname)\nax.set_xlabel(\"tumor area / image area\")\nax.set_ylabel(\"images\")\nax.legend()\nplt.show()\n\nmanifest.groupby(\"label_name\")[\"tumor_frac\"].describe(percentiles=[0.05, 0.5, 0.95]).reindex(CLASS_NAMES).round(4)\n# TODO: revisit localization_threshold once I see how Grad-CAM blobs compare to the 5th-percentile tumors","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-16T07:28:19.990564Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-16T07:28:19.990958Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-16T07:28:20.322212Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-10-16T07:28:19.990921Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-16T07:28:20.321493Z"}},"outputs":[{"output_type":"display_data","data":{"text/plain":"<Figure size 700x350 with 1 Axes>","image/png":"\n"},"metadata":{}},{"execution_count":59,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"             count    mean     std     min      5%     50%     95%     max\nlabel_name                                                                \nmeningioma   708.0  0.0178  0.0119  0.0010  0.0032  0.0152  0.0427  0.0581\nglioma      1426.0  0.0220  0.0154  0.0011  0.0038  0.0184  0.0526  0.0971\npituitary    930.0  0.0084  0.0063  0.0012  0.0021  0.0067  0.0202  0.0446","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\n